==============================================================================
TEST 1 - THE COUNTING ARGUMENT (pigeonhole principle)
==============================================================================
Claim under test: 'a good enough algorithm could shrink any file'.
Method: compress 4,000 independent random 256-byte strings with the five
strongest general-purpose codecs available, keeping the best result each time.

  Strings compressed        : 4,000
  Strings that got SMALLER  : 0  (0.000%)
  Total input               : 1,024,000 bytes
  Total output (best-of-5)  : 1,064,000 bytes
  Net result                : +3.91%  <- compression EXPANDED the data

  Why this can never be beaten:
  There are 2^2048 possible 256-byte strings but only 2^2048 - 1 shorter strings.
  Any injective (reversible) map must therefore leave at least one input un-shrunk.
  Extended: at most 1/256 of inputs can shrink by even one byte, at most 1/65536
  by two bytes. Compression is a redistribution of length, never a net reduction.

==============================================================================
TEST 2 - RECURSIVE COMPRESSION (the 'infinite squeeze' claim)
==============================================================================
Claim under test: 'feed the output back in and keep shrinking'.
Method: take real text, compress, then re-compress the output 12 times.

  pass  0 (original)    2,000,000 B
  pass  1                188,092 B   -1,811,908 vs previous
  pass  2                188,164 B   +72 vs previous
  pass  3                188,236 B   +72 vs previous
  pass  4                188,308 B   +72 vs previous
  pass  5                188,380 B   +72 vs previous
  pass  6                188,452 B   +72 vs previous
  pass  7                188,524 B   +72 vs previous
  pass  8                188,588 B   +64 vs previous
  pass  9                188,660 B   +72 vs previous
  pass 10                188,732 B   +72 vs previous
  pass 11                188,804 B   +72 vs previous
  pass 12                188,876 B   +72 vs previous

  First pass:  -90.6%   (real structure removed)
  Passes 2-12: +0.417%   (output is now incompressible; only overhead accrues)

==============================================================================
TEST 3 - HOW FAR ARE REAL FILES FROM *THEIR OWN* ENTROPY FLOOR?
==============================================================================
This is the number that matters. The wall is real, but real files sit nowhere
near it. Empirical order-N entropy vs what today's best codecs actually achieve.

  file                           o0     o1     o2     o3 |    xz -9e   zstd19    br 11
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  text_english.txt             5.16   3.75   2.45   1.44 |     0.791    0.821    0.747
  code_python.py               4.66   3.50   2.42   1.62 |     1.564    1.600    1.533
  data_xml.xml                 5.35   3.05   1.57   0.90 |     0.839    0.909    0.803
  data_records.json            4.65   2.52   1.42   1.11 |     0.914    0.923    0.893
  data_records.csv             5.05   3.40   2.40   2.07 |     2.280    2.325    2.234

  Read this carefully: the best codecs already beat order-3 entropy, because they
  model far longer contexts. Order-N estimates are an upper bound, not the floor.
  The true floor (Kolmogorov complexity) is UNCOMPUTABLE - which means nobody,
  including the market leaders, can prove they are near it. That unprovable gap
  is the entire commercial opportunity.

==============================================================================
TEST 4 - SIZING THE REAL OPPORTUNITY
==============================================================================
If the wall can't move, where does a better product come from? Two places.

  (a) STRUCTURE THE GENERAL-PURPOSE CODECS CANNOT SEE
      A .docx / .xlsx / .zip / .apk is a container of DEFLATE streams. To a
      byte-oriented compressor it looks like noise. Unwrap it first:

      office_sheet.xlsx          934,443 B  -> xz direct   880,464 ( 94.2%)  -> unwrapped   531,592 ( 56.9%)   gain  39.6%
      container_wheel.zip      7,233,653 B  -> xz direct 7,220,208 ( 99.8%)  -> unwrapped 3,805,096 ( 52.6%)   gain  47.3%
      office_report.docx          45,763 B  -> xz direct    45,496 ( 99.4%)  -> unwrapped    31,032 ( 67.8%)   gain  31.8%

  (b) NUMERIC LAYOUT THE CODECS CANNOT SEE
      Float/int arrays are byte-interleaved. Transpose them and the exponent
      bytes line up into a highly repetitive plane:

      data_floats.bin          7,200,000 B  -> xz direct 5,576,656 ( 77.5%)  -> shuffled 5,267,188 ( 73.2%)
      data_timeseries.bin      8,000,000 B  -> xz direct 1,103,940 ( 13.8%)  -> shuffled 1,020,920 ( 12.8%)  -> delta+shuffle 1,032,652 ( 12.9%)
      image_raw16.bin          2,880,000 B  -> xz direct 2,203,304 ( 76.5%)  -> shuffled 2,116,328 ( 73.5%)  -> delta+shuffle 2,396,684 ( 83.2%)

==============================================================================
CONCLUSION: the wall is real and immovable. The gap between the wall and where
today's mainstream tools actually stop is large, exploitable, and is where Crush
will live. No physics was harmed in the making of this compressor.
